<office:body><office:text>
<text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_1" text:outline-level="1">The word</text:h>
<text:p text:style-name="Standard">The wrath of Achilles is what the poem is of <text:bookmark-start text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_Qw3Er5Ty7Ui9"/>(Nagy 1979, 73)<text:bookmark-end text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_Qw3Er5Ty7Ui9"/>, as is often said.</text:p>
<text:p text:style-name="Standard">Much is written of it <text:bookmark-start text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_As2Df4Gh6Jk8"/>(see Nagy 1979, chap. 2; West 1988; 1960, 12 and elsewhere)<text:bookmark-end text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_As2Df4Gh6Jk8"/>. The singer <text:bookmark-start text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_Zx1Cv3Bn5Mq7"/>(Lord, <text:span text:style-name="T1">The Singer of Tales</text:span>, 12)<text:bookmark-end text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_Zx1Cv3Bn5Mq7"/> is another matter.</text:p>
<text:p text:style-name="Standard">Not all agree.<text:note text:id="ftn1" text:note-class="footnote"><text:note-citation>1</text:note-citation><text:note-body><text:p text:style-name="Footnote">See <text:bookmark-start text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_Pl0Ok9Ij8Uh7"/>Nagy, <text:span text:style-name="T1">The Best of the Achaeans</text:span>, 73<text:bookmark-end text:name="ZOTERO_BREF_Pl0Ok9Ij8Uh7"/>; but he says otherwise elsewhere.</text:p></text:note-body></text:note> And so it stands.</text:p>
<text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_1" text:outline-level="1">Works</text:h>
<text:p text:style-name="Bibliography_20_1">Lord, Albert B. 1960. <text:span text:style-name="T1">The Singer of Tales</text:span>. Cambridge, Mass.: Harvard University Press.</text:p>
<text:p text:style-name="Bibliography_20_1">Nagy, Gregory. 1979. <text:span text:style-name="T1">The Best of the Achaeans: Concepts of the Hero in Archaic Greek Poetry</text:span>. Baltimore: Johns Hopkins University Press.</text:p>
<text:p text:style-name="Bibliography_20_1">West, M. L. 1988. ‘The Rise of the Greek Epic’. <text:span text:style-name="T1">The Journal of Hellenic Studies</text:span> 108: 151–72.</text:p>
</office:text></office:body></office:document>
